<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 9 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">How Empires Fall</h1><p class="path-tagline">Invasion, division, bankruptcy and revolution: the many ways great powers come to an end.</p><p class="path-intro">No empire lasts for ever, but they end in strikingly different ways. Some erode slowly until a final blow merely confirms what has already happened; others are destroyed in a single catastrophe. This path compares the fall of the Western Roman Empire, the Mongol destruction of Abbasid Baghdad, the Ottoman conquest of Constantinople, the collapse of Qing China and the dissolution of the Ottoman Empire itself, asking what internal weakness and external pressure each contributed.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Strategist</a> · <a href="/start">the Timekeeper</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/roman-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-caesar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · 27 BCE – 476 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Roman Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Adrianople, the Visigothic sack of 410 and the loss of North Africa stripped the Western Empire of armies, prestige and revenue.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-410"><span class="path-step-ph">2</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Event · 410 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Sack of Rome (410)</span><span class="path-step-why">Alaric's Visigoths shattered the myth of Roman invulnerability, though the Western Empire limped on for another sixty-six years.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-kitab-al-mukhtasar-fi-hisab-al-jabr-wal-muqabala.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · 750 CE – 1517 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Abbasid Caliphate</span><span class="path-step-why">Once the commercial hub of the known world, the caliphate had long ceased to be a first-rank military power when the Mongols arrived.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-baghdad-1258"><span class="path-step-ph">4</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1258</span><span class="path-step-name">Siege of Baghdad (1258)</span><span class="path-step-why">The Mongol destruction of Baghdad in 1258 ended the caliphate there, although a shadow caliphate survived under the Mamluks in Cairo.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/byzantine-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-arab-siege-of-constantinople.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · 330 CE – 1453 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Byzantine Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">A civilisation that saw its own decline clearly: the loss of Anatolia and the crusader sack of 1204 did damage it never repaired.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-constantinople"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fall-of-constantinople.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1453</span><span class="path-step-name">Fall of Constantinople</span><span class="path-step-why">On 29 May 1453 Mehmed II's cannon breached the Theodosian walls, extinguishing the Byzantine Empire after eleven centuries.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/qing-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/opium-wars.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · 1644 CE – 1912 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Qing Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Fiscal strain, rebellions such as the Taiping and defeat in the Opium Wars hollowed out China's last dynasty from within and without.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/xinhai-revolution"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xinhai-revolution.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1911</span><span class="path-step-name">Xinhai Revolution</span><span class="path-step-why">An uprising at Wuchang in 1911 spread from province to province and led to Puyi's abdication, ending two thousand years of imperial rule.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/ottoman-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suleymaniye-mosque.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Profile · 1299 CE – 1922 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Ottoman Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">The conqueror of Constantinople endured until defeat in the First World War and the abolition of the sultanate in 1922.</span></span></a></li></ol>
